---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../../components/Screenshot.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Sentry" activeNav="/platform/integrations/sentry">
  <h1>Sentry</h1>
  <p>
    Sentry is a compatible event relay for errors and traces. Create one routed DSN, attach one or
    more application targets to it, and the platform resolves each incoming envelope to the right
    application. The original envelope is retained for inspection and can optionally be forwarded to
    an upstream Sentry-compatible DSN.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="integrations-sentry"
    alt="Sentry integration tab with a customer telemetry relay, two routed application targets, relay status and recent errors and traces"
    caption="A routed DSN can serve more than one application target while preserving a per-target DSN for SDK setup."
  />

  <h2>Create a routed DSN</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Workspace settings → Integrations → Sentry</strong> and select
    <strong>New routed DSN</strong>. Give it a recognizable name, choose how events identify their
    application, and add at least one target. A target is an application by itself or one specific
    package identifier / web domain belonging to that application.
  </p>
  <p>
    The platform gives every target its own compatible DSN. Use that target DSN in the Sentry SDK
    when an application has one fixed destination. Use the shared DSN when a single SDK endpoint must
    resolve between multiple targets.
  </p>

  <h3>Routing methods</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Where the values come from</th><th>Use it when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Query</td><td>Query parameters on the envelope request</td><td>Your transport can add stable request parameters.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Headers</td><td>HTTP headers on the envelope request</td><td>A server-side transport or proxy controls request headers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event metadata</td><td>The Sentry event's <code>contexts</code>, <code>extra</code>, <code>tags</code>, or <code>attributes</code></td><td>The SDK already knows the application at runtime.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Configure an application-slug key and, when targets share an application, an identifier key. The
    identifier value can match either the exact package identifier / web domain or its platform
    (<code>ios</code>, <code>android</code>, or <code>web</code>). A shared DSN must resolve to exactly
    one configured target; otherwise the envelope is rejected rather than being attributed to the
    wrong application.
  </p>

  <h3>Routing from SDK metadata</h3>
  <p>
    With <strong>Event metadata</strong> and source <strong>Contexts</strong>, the following JavaScript
    setup matches the default keys <code>app.slug</code> and <code>app.identifier</code>. The same two
    values may instead be supplied through <code>setExtra</code>, <code>setTag</code>, or
    <code>setAttribute</code> when that source is selected (or when the source is <strong>Auto</strong>).
  </p>
  <pre><code>import * as Sentry from '@sentry/node';

Sentry.init(&#123; dsn: 'https://TARGET_KEY@your-app-manager.example/PROJECT_ID' &#125;);

Sentry.setContext('app', &#123;
  slug: 'customer-app',
  identifier: 'customer.acme.example', // package ID or web domain
&#125;);</code></pre>
  <p>
    Use your copied DSN exactly as shown. Never put an upstream DSN or secret token in client-side
    code; upstream forwarding is configured only in the platform.
  </p>

  <h2>Inspect errors and traces on the application</h2>
  <p>
    Each routed event appears on the target application's <strong>Errors &amp; traces</strong> page,
    alongside builds, files, and media. Repeated errors are grouped into one Issue by their Sentry
    fingerprint, while every original occurrence remains available for inspection. Transactions stay
    in a separate trace stream and remain connected through their trace ID.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="telemetry-errors-traces"
    alt="Customer App errors and traces triage page with Issue inbox, Trace stream, filters and search"
    caption="The application page is a compact inbox: filter, search, then open the Issue or trace that needs investigation."
  />

  <p>
    Filter the Issue inbox or trace stream by environment and one or more app identifiers or domains.
    Opening an Issue or trace takes you to a dedicated investigation page, where you can switch
    between its occurrences/events without leaving the case. It shows the exact exception and source
    context, request, user, contexts and extra data, tags, and raw JSON. Tabs without data are omitted.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="telemetry-issue-detail"
    alt="Dedicated Issue investigation page with an occurrence rail and full Sentry event payload"
    caption="Each case keeps its event timeline beside the complete payload, so the inbox stays focused on triage."
  />

  <h2>Forward to an upstream DSN</h2>
  <p>
    Enable <strong>Relay to upstream Sentry DSN</strong> in the DSN form and enter an optional
    Sentry-compatible upstream DSN. After the platform stores and resolves the envelope, it forwards
    the untouched envelope asynchronously. A forwarding failure does not remove the locally captured
    event; the relay status and consecutive failures remain visible on the Sentry integration tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lets an existing Sentry or Better Stack workflow continue unchanged while the platform adds
    application-level errors and traces.
  </p>
  <p>
    Automation can manage the same DSNs and inspect the same cases through the
    <a href="/cli/integrations/sentry"><code>appmctl sentry</code> and <code>appmctl telemetry</code> commands</a>.
    The Web UI and API share their request validation, so routing rules behave identically in both surfaces.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
